    <h2 style="font-size: 20pt;">Events and Initiatives at the Centers of Excellence</h2>
    <p style="font-size: 11pt;">The Center for Ethics and Social Jus-tice is piloting the innovative new Polis Program in philosophy, theology, and service-learning, a year-long set of team-taught courses. People interested in learning more should go to the Center for Ethics website: http://www.luc.edu/ethics/</p>
    <p style="font-size: 11pt;">The Center for the Human Rights of Children is collaborating with the American Academy of Pediatrics to bring "The Voice of Afri-can Children: The Midnight Hour" to October 30, they hosted a fundraiser for the Center, titled "The Night-mare of Immigration Politics."</p>
    <p style="font-size: 11pt;">The Center for Science and Math Edu-cation has worked with the School of Education to launch the new MED in Sci-ence Education, with a specialization in chemistry teaching. They are also hard at work improving science and math edu-cation throughout Chicago.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 11pt;">The Center for Urban Environmental Research and Policy will be involved in the upcoming Hunger Week activities, and they recently named a new Sustain-ability Initiative Director, Dr. Marshall Eames, from the Department of Natural Science, will start his new position in January.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 11pt;">Learning is excited to announce the launch of their new e-journal, Gateways: International Journal of Community Re-search and Engagement. The journal is co-sponsored by the University of Tech-nology Sydney Sportfest. Please visit http://www.luc.edu/curts/ for more in-formation.</p>
    <div style="clear: both;"></div>
    <h3 style="font-size: 16pt; margin-top: 25px;">Loyola Receives Royalties from GlaxoSmithKline</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 11pt;">The university recently received a check from GlaxoSmithKline (GSK) for ${{ royalty_amount }} for innovations made more than ten years ago by three Loyola faculty members and a Loyola student.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 11pt;">The main U.S. patent is # {{ patent_number }}, and there are a number of foreign patent applications pending. The technology is currently licensed to GSK, and they have used it to develop a vaccine against papilloma viruses in humans (HPV). Sales in for-eign countries have been very good, which accounts for the large royalty check received. Sales in the U.S. are expected following FDA approval of the vaccine, which should be finalized next year.</p>
    <p style="font-size: 11pt;">ORs has entered into an agreement with Tremonti, Inc. to provide consultation on whether and how to pursue protection of the vaccine made by faculty on the lakeside cam-pus. This agreement is similar to one that Tremonti already has with the medi-cal center campus. If you think your re-search has or could result in a novel process, device, composition of matter, or any kind of pat-entable subject matter, please contact the ORS Director who can put you in touch with Tremonti for expert advice.</p>
